ethods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Secure rule vs short fit.","caption":"Eighteen items.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">−10 pattern</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">next 60</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">two rules fit briefly</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fluency with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTwo different rules that fit only the first three terms are not secure until checked further. Here is a more productive model: Set: continue −4 pattern; complete ×5 table; write a rule from 2,5,8,11. Prefer a named rule over a single guess.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSubtract 10; next 60.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Two different rules that fit only the first three terms are not secure until checked further. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fluency with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPattern fluency practises stating rules, extending sequences and reading input–output tables accurately. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 17, Lesson 4: Fluency: patterns","unit_number":5,"unit_title":"Patterns, number sentences and money","week":17,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSet: continue −4 pattern; complete ×5 table; write a rule from 2,5,8,11. Prefer a named rule over a single guess.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Pattern fluency practice.","caption":"Verify rule further along.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fluency</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">continue; table; rule</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Check</te